Acne vulgaris
Young Adult & Adolecence
Comedones/Papules/Pustules, Seborrhoea, Facial/Truncal distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-002
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful, diffuse truncal and facial pustular eruption causing severe discomfort and inability to wear standard clothing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapidly spreading, painful pustular and nodulocystic rash over the trunk and face causing acute functional impairment and severe distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to exclude systemic infectious etiologies (e.g., disseminated varicella or staphylococcal scalded skin) and to initiate prompt localized and systemic therapy to prevent clinical deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back and face are covered in weeping, painful blind pimples that feel like they are burning through my skin."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a rapidly progressive exacerbation of inflammatory lesions over the past 72 hours, predominantly affecting the upper back and face. The patient reports a background of mild oily skin, but recently developed widespread erythematous papules and pustules. These have evolved into exquisitely tender nodulocystic lesions that weep serosanguinous fluid when irritated by clothing. The intense pain causes severe psychosocial distress, lowered self-esteem, and sleep disturbance. Post-inflammatory erythema is marked.
Functional Status: Independent, but movement is restricted due to friction pain on the upper back.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychological distress impacting relationship dynamics and occupational attendance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Mildly diaphoretic, anxious, leaning forward while seated to prevent contact between his back and the chair.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals extensive seborrhoea. There is a dense facial and truncal distribution of open and closed comedones. Superimposed on this are numerous coalescing erythematous papules, pustules, and deep, tender nodulocystic lesions, particularly over the scapulae and central chest. Several lesions display superficial excoriation with mild serous exudate. No localized fluctuant abscess requiring immediate drainage. Lymphadenopathy is absent.
Environmental Observations: Poor ventilation in the bedroom; patient utilizes highly scented, aggressive physical exfoliants visible in the bathroom which are exacerbating the dermal barrier breakdown.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Exacerbation of Severe Acne Vulgaris (Truncal predominant) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pityrosporum Folliculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distinct presence of comedones and nodulocystic lesions, rather than a uniform monomorphic pruritic papular eruption.

Eosinophilic Folliculitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of intense pruritus and absence of immunocompromise risk factors, alongside typical pubertal/hormonal acne morphology.

Secondary Syphilis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of palmar/plantar involvement, and the clearly localized seborrhoeic distribution of comedones and cysts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibited an acutely painful, rapidly worsening truncal and facial nodulocystic eruption that severely limited basic physical function (wearing clothing, sitting, sleeping). The high index of suspicion for rapidly advancing secondary bacterial infection, coupled with the patient's acute psychological distress and functional impairment, necessitated an urgent after-hours home visit. A delay in assessment until routine hours would have prolonged severe pain, increased the risk of deeper dermal abscess formation requiring surgical intervention, and caused immense psychological harm. Immediate evaluation allowed for proper diagnostic differentiation and prompt initiation of targeted antibiotic therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Cephalexin 500mg orally four times daily for 5 days to cover suspected acute secondary staphylococcal overgrowth, followed by transition to Doxycycline by the regular GP. Recommended immediate cessation of aggressive mechanical exfoliants.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce dermal barrier damage, switch to a gentle, pH-balanced liquid cleanser using bare hands rather than abrasive scrubs, observing that intact skin heals faster and hurts less. To manage stress and friction, wear loose, 100% cotton clothing, which immediately reduces physical irritation and limits the weeping of active cysts.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 3 to 5 days to assess the resolution of acute secondary inflammatory changes and establish a long-term dermatological plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department if the patient develops rigors, a fever above 38.5 degrees Celsius, an expanding area of red, hot, swollen skin tracking away from the acne lesions, or signs of systemic toxicity.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This represents an acute inflammatory and likely secondarily infected flare of underlying acne; ongoing specialized management will be required.
Clinical Course: The acute burning pain and weeping should improve within 48 to 72 hours of starting the antibiotics, though the underlying cysts will take weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest ED if you develop a high temperature, uncontrollable shivering, or if the redness on your back rapidly spreads and becomes hot to the touch within the next 24 to 48 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The prescribed antibiotics are targeted to rapidly kill the secondary bacteria that are causing the intense weeping, burning, and rapid swelling of your acne spots.
Actionable Steps: Stop using all rough scrubs immediately to let your skin barrier heal. Wear loose cotton clothing to prevent friction and allow your skin to breathe, which will immediately reduce your pain.

